==Russian==
{{wikipedia|lang=ru}}

===Etymology 1===
{{der|ru|orv|Донъ}}, perhaps from {{der|ru|ae|𐬛𐬁𐬥𐬎|t=river}}, from {{der|ru|ira-pro|*dáHnu|*dānu}}, from {{der|ru|iir-pro|*dáHnu}}, from {{der|ru|ine-pro|*déh₂nu}}.

Cognate with {{cog|uk|Дін}}, {{cog|grc|Τάναϊς}}, {{cog|os|дон||river}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-дон.ogg}}

===Proper noun===
{{ru-proper noun+|loc=Дону́|n=s|adj=донско́й}}

# {{place|ru|a <<river>>, the fifth-longest in [[Europe]], <<c/Russia>>|t=Don}} running from north to south down into the [[Azov Sea]] through [[Rostov-na-Donu|Rostov]] and [[Voronezh]]. The home of the Don [[Cossack]]s

====Declension====
{{ru-noun-table|loc=Дону́|n=s}}

{{c|ru|Places in Tula Oblast}}
{{c|ru|Places in Lipetsk Oblast}}
{{c|ru|Places in Volgograd Oblast}}
{{c|ru|Places in Rostov Oblast}}

===References===
* Vasmer, Max (1950). Russisches etymologisches Wörterbuch [Etymological Dictionary of the Russian Language] (in German). Heidelberg: Carl Winter.

===Etymology 2===
Probably from the common {{der|en|cel|-}} river name {{der|en|cel-pro|*Dānu}} << {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰenh₂-}}, found in many other river names such as {{m|en|Danube}}, and {{m|en|Dniester}}.  Connected with the Welsh river goddess {{w|Dôn}}, whose name is from the same origin, though it could have been influenced by {{cog|cy|dawn||gift}}, {{cog|ga|dán||gift, offering}}, {{cog|la|dōnum}}.

===Proper noun===
{{ru-proper noun+|n=s}}

# {{translit|ru|en|Don}}.

